WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
Jason Statham has been beating up bad&nbsp;
guys for twenty years. Ever wondered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.520
which of his movements stand out among&nbsp;
the rest? Was it a “Fast &amp; Furious” fight?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.440
An impromptu life-or-death love&nbsp;
scene? Keep watching to find out. 
 
 

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.320
Everyone who's seen "The Fast and the&nbsp;
Furious: Tokyo Drift," the third entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.720
in the "Fast &amp; Furious" saga, knows that the&nbsp;
following movies, from "The Fast and the Furious"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.240
to "Fast &amp; Furious 6," are prequels. They all&nbsp;
lead up to Han's inevitable retirement and death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.040
as seen in the climactic car chase in "Tokyo&nbsp;
Drift." Still, when the post-credits sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:39.040
of "Fast &amp; Furious 6" revisits Han's fatal car&nbsp;
crash, nobody expects the revelation that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.400
death was no accident: It was murder! What's&nbsp;
more, the killer is revealed to be none other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.560
than Jason Statham's Deckard Shaw, setting the&nbsp;
stage for him to be a major threat in "Furious 7."

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:56.720
While this retcon of Han's death is itself&nbsp;
retconned in "F9," which reveals he faked his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.600
death with the help of Mr. Nobody, the impact&nbsp;
of Statham's surprise appearance and the hype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.600
it brought to "Furious 7" cannot be overstated.&nbsp;
Dominic Toretto and his family are being hunted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:13.360
a bloodthirsty assassin, and it'll take all their&nbsp;
skills to survive the wrath of Deckard Shaw.
 
 

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.440
Jason Statham may be in a league of his own&nbsp;
when it comes to action superstardom, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.800
he's not the only hunky Brit with&nbsp;
impressive high kicks. Enter Scott Adkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.000
star of such films as "Universal Soldier: Day&nbsp;
of Reckoning" and "Triple Threat." Adkins has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.920
earned a reputation as one of the toughest&nbsp;
action stars out there, for very good reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.840
Naturally, it was only a matter of time before&nbsp;
he and Statham went toe-to-toe in single combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.720
That opportunity finally arrived&nbsp;
in 2012, with "The Expendables 2."

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.680
While Sylvester Stallone's Barney Ross fights the&nbsp;
main villain, played by Jean-Claude Van Damme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.640
Statham's character, Lee Christmas, fights Van&nbsp;
Damme's lieutenant, Hector, played by Adkins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.920
Hector has a knife, while Statham is armed with&nbsp;
knuckle-dusters, and the two square off in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:05.120
bloody duel. While he takes a few hits along the&nbsp;
way, Christmas eventually gains the upper hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.080
ultimately punching Hector into the rear rotor&nbsp;
of a helicopter. Rather than being turned into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.600
a pink mist, the impact is portrayed more&nbsp;
realistically. The rotor is destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.880
though not before it rips off Hector's head&nbsp;
and one of his arms. It's a grisly sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.760
but it only appears on screen for a fraction&nbsp;
of a second. Hardcore gore-hounds will find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.480
themselves going through it frame-by-frame,&nbsp;
to better admire the R-rated carnage.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.400
“Can’t beat a classic” 
 
 

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.440
Directed by the dynamic duo of&nbsp;
Mark Neveldine and Brian Taylor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:43.120
the "Crank" movies specialize in sensory&nbsp;
overload and testosterone-fueled audacity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.240
They're effectively parodies&nbsp;
of over-the-top action movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.200
what with their steadily diminishing sense of&nbsp;
reality and open mockery of the audience. As a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.120
result, these movies aren't for everyone. Are you&nbsp;
interested in watching an absurdly unhinged Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.880
Statham kick butt while surrounded by characters&nbsp;
who mix R-rated caricature with Saturday morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.880
cartoons? If so, you will enjoy the wild ride&nbsp;
offered by "Crank" and "Crank: High Voltage."

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.360
While "Crank" starts out realistically enough, its&nbsp;
stylistic sensibilities slowly overtake the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.520
in ways both subtle and overt. As the movies go&nbsp;
on, the characters become increasingly self-aware:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.080
The finale of "Crank: High Voltage" has&nbsp;
Statham's character Chev Chelios stare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.280
at the camera and raise his middle finger&nbsp;
directly at the viewer while on fire and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.000
standing next to a swimming pool, no less.&nbsp;
Like we said, it's not for everyone. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.080
the "Crank" movies are worth watching, at least&nbsp;
for these moments of sheer unpredictability.
 
 

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.000
The "Crank" movies are arguably more remembered&nbsp;
for their sex scenes than for their action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.440
sequences. This makes sense: The premise of&nbsp;
these movies is that protagonist Chev Chelios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.000
must keep his heart rate up, or else the poison&nbsp;
in his body will kill him. One way he keeps his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.560
heart going is by having public trysts with&nbsp;
his girlfriend, Eve, played by Amy Smart.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.360
These scenes, present in both movies,&nbsp;
would be unwatchable and disturbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:11.520
if it weren't for the surreal tone of&nbsp;
these films. In the world of "Crank,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.240
public expressions of affection, be they on a&nbsp;
horse racetrack or in the middle of the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.040
are just part of the formula. As the action&nbsp;
sequences are obscene and ridiculous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.800
the romance scenes need to maintain&nbsp;
the same level of bizarre energy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.320
To that end, "Crank" is successful in achieving&nbsp;
what it sets out to accomplish. You'll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.080
forget watching Statham and Smart frantically&nbsp;
create friction on a set of stadium stairs.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.640
“Oh god, what are you looking at?”
 
 
 
When a giant prehistoric shark declares war on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.560
humanity and starts eating everyone in sight, only&nbsp;
one man — and a bunch of supporting characters —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:55.200
can fight back. That man is Jason Statham. "The&nbsp;
Meg," released in 2018, follows Statham as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:00.560
does battle with the gigantic shark while trying&nbsp;
to protect his comrades and innocent beachgoers.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.640
This wild film has its fair share of PG-13 terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.960
though director Jon Turteltaub originally&nbsp;
intended for the film to be a blood-soaked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.000
R-rated gore-fest. Nevertheless, a decent&nbsp;
amount of nastiness remains in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.480
much of it due to the fact that the marine&nbsp;
life does a lot of the bleeding, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.840
unlucky humans. Ultimately, the Megalodon shark is&nbsp;
killed after Statham's character cuts it open with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.120
his underwater vehicle and stabs its eye with a&nbsp;
harpoon. Drawn to the scent of blood in the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:36.000
a swarm of smaller sharks rush to the scene and&nbsp;
devour the "meg" in a bloody mess. Thus, nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.360
corrects itself and finally eradicates a species&nbsp;
that should have died out millions of years ago.
 
 

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.320
One of the first scenes in Michael Mann's 2004&nbsp;
masterpiece "Collateral" sees Tom Cruise's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.600
mysterious character pick up a briefcase from&nbsp;
a balding man in a dapper suit, played by Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.480
Statham. He only appears in a single scene, but&nbsp;
he dresses exactly like his character in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.840
Transporter," Frank Martin, does. Some viewers&nbsp;
speculate that he may be playing Martin himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.880
making this a subtle crossover. Others believe&nbsp;
his casting is an allusion to his famous role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.360
but there's no real intended connection&nbsp;
between "Collateral" and "The Transporter."

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.200
Either way, Statham's presence helps set&nbsp;
the tone for "Collateral," which stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.040
as one of Mann's finest films. Its gritty&nbsp;
realism and incredible digital photography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.440
give it a unique look that has rarely&nbsp;
been matched by any other filmmaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.080
save for Mann himself. Meanwhile, Tom&nbsp;
Cruise and Jamie Foxx offer fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.920
performances that explore masculinity and duty&nbsp;
and culminate in one heck of a subway showdown.
 
 

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.840
The entire "Expendables" franchise is&nbsp;
full of memorable action sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.160
many of which feature Jason Statham. From&nbsp;
taking down bad guys with throwing knives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.240
to beating up a squad of middle-aged&nbsp;
basketball players, Statham kicks all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:58.000
kinds of butt in these movies. Perhaps his&nbsp;
most memorable moment in the 2010 first film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:04.240
comes when he and Stallone take down a bunch of&nbsp;
soldiers, via an absurdly enormous explosion.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.040
After escaping in their jalopy of a plane, they&nbsp;
decide they're not going to leave without causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:13.840
a bona fide spectacle. Stallone turns the&nbsp;
plane around while Statham goes through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:19.520
secret compartment and emerges out of a hole&nbsp;
on the plane's nose. After Stallone dumps fuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.160
all over the pier from which they escaped,&nbsp;
Statham uses a flare gun to ignite it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:30.400
This engulfs the dock in a massive fireball,&nbsp;
killing a great many soldiers in the process.
 
 

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:37.360
Directed by Paul W.S. Anderson, "Death Race,"&nbsp;
a loose remake of the 1975 cult classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.400
"Death Race 2000," stars Jason Statham as&nbsp;
a prison inmate forced to compete in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:48.640
eponymous Death Race. It's a no-holds-barred&nbsp;
vehicular combat challenge that plays out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.680
like an R-rated take on "Mario Kart"&nbsp;
or "Crash Team Racing," complete with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.800
on-track power-ups that unlock the vehicles'&nbsp;
weapons. While Statham takes part in his fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.000
share of hand-to-hand fights, the real draw&nbsp;
here is the "Twisted Metal"-style mayhem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.520
which Anderson, always a reliable&nbsp;
action director, delivers in spades.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.480
"Death Race" has no shortage of grisly&nbsp;
kills and outrageous car wrecks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:20.400
By far the film's stand-out moment comes when&nbsp;
Statham and allied driver Machine Gun Joe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.240
played by Tyrese Gibson, are forced to take&nbsp;
on the Dreadnought. It’s a massive vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:31.520
armed with heavy machine guns, flamethrowers,&nbsp;
and impenetrable armor. They knock it off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.920
board by tricking it into colliding with&nbsp;
an immovable obstacle at terminal speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.960
causing it to flip end over end and throw its&nbsp;
passengers into the air. This amazing crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:47.280
is captured in full, and delivers exactly what&nbsp;
viewers want from a movie called "Death Race."
 
 

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.840
There are debates to be had as to what movie&nbsp;
contains the best Jason Statham fight scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:59.600
However, there is no debate as to which movie&nbsp;
contains the most sensual Jason Statham fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.640
scene. That honor belongs to "The Transporter,"&nbsp;
in which a shirtless Frank Martin kicks over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.720
multiple tubs of grease, covers his shirtless&nbsp;
body with the slippery goo, and proceeds to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:16.000
engage in a shirtless battle with half-a-dozen&nbsp;
bad guys. Did we mention he's shirtless?

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:22.080
Thanks to veteran action director Corey Yuen, this&nbsp;
fight has wonderful kinetic energy. Much humor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.080
and bone-crunching excitement comes from&nbsp;
the henchmen's attempts to grab Statham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.320
who is too slippery to hold.&nbsp;
It's a truly unique action scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:36.080
full of visual invention and delightful&nbsp;
stunts that give the viewer an appreciation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:43.520
of the human body's ability to perform incredible&nbsp;
feats. Also, we cannot emphasize this enough:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:50.160
He's shirtless and covered in grease. You&nbsp;
don't see that in many action-thrillers.
 
 

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.600
"Hatchet Harry is a man you pay, if you owe."&nbsp;
Those words are spoken by Jason Statham in 1998's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.760
"Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels" when&nbsp;
the protagonists consider the possibility of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.720
not paying the money they owe Hatchet Harry.&nbsp;
Statham relays the story of Smithy Robinson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.240
who failed to pay his debt or offer a reasonable&nbsp;
explanation as to why he couldn't. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:15.280
Hatchet Harry reached for whatever&nbsp;
was on his desk and bludgeoned poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:21.760
Smithy to death with it. That object just so&nbsp;
happened to be an immodestly sized sex toy.

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:27.600
The sight of Harry going berserk on&nbsp;
Smithy is as absurd as it is intimidating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:32.480
and the idea of him doing something similar&nbsp;
to the protagonists is enough to inform their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:38.080
motivations for the rest of the movie. Statham's&nbsp;
narration of this infamous episode, filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:44.720
seeming admiration at Harry's audacity, elevates&nbsp;
the scene to a whole other level. It's hilarious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:51.200
memorable, and let 1998 audiences know that&nbsp;
Jason Statham was going to do great things.
 
 

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:55.520
There is no shortage of fight&nbsp;
scenes in the "Crank" films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:01.120
from bloody shoot-outs to brutal fisticuffs,&nbsp;
and even a midair brawl in the skies above Los&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:05.840
Angeles. But the best part of these&nbsp;
movies is their unpredictability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:11.600
The viewer honestly has no clue what's going to&nbsp;
happen next at any given point. Nowhere is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:16.560
more evident than in the downright absurd&nbsp;
Godzilla fight in "Crank: High Voltage."

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.600
Chev Chelios has fought hundreds of bad guys&nbsp;
by the time he faces off against Johnny Vang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:27.440
in an electrical power station. As such, the movie&nbsp;
doesn't indulge in a traditional fight. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:32.400
the scene suddenly shifts, taking on the&nbsp;
aesthetic of a grainy Japanese kaiju movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:37.760
Chelios is inexplicably portrayed as a giant&nbsp;
"Chev-Zilla," with Vang transformed into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:44.080
equally gigantic monstrosity. The two proceed to&nbsp;
engage in a fight that pays homage to any given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:49.120
"Godzilla versus" movie, complete with absurd&nbsp;
wire stunts and sets that look like they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:54.960
made of cardboard. It makes no sense, and it's&nbsp;
one of the best sequences in the whole movie.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:56.240
“Don’t even think about it!” 
 
 

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:01.840
From the moment Jason Statham's character is&nbsp;
introduced, audiences know it will all come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:06.240
down to a one-on-one, hand-to-hand grudge&nbsp;
match between Deckard Shaw and Dominic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:11.040
Toretto. When the time comes in "Furious&nbsp;
7," their inevitable confrontation does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:16.400
disappoint. While the fisticuffs are firmly&nbsp;
in the realm of PG-13 action, the hits are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:22.080
nevertheless brutal. These characters pummel each&nbsp;
other with the full force of their desires for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.960
vengeance — Deckard for his injured brother,&nbsp;
Owen, and Toretto for his fallen friend, Han.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:31.200
The fight is pretty close throughout,&nbsp;
and beautifully choreographed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:38.000
maximum impact. Gravity-defying suplexes and&nbsp;
absurd flying elbows somehow feel visceral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:43.600
and believable here, even though no&nbsp;
human could ever survive the hits these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:49.280
two titans of testosterone endure. The battle&nbsp;
culminates with Vin Diesel's unforgettable line.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:55.600
"The thing about street fights&nbsp;
... the street always wins."

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.640
Like most of "Furious 7," that&nbsp;
line barely makes sense, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:03.120
it's so sincere that you can't help but love it.

